WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:00.600
Hi.

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:04.050
Today I want to talk about
learning a language in one month.

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:09.150
Um, if you enjoy these videos, please
subscribe, click on the bell to get

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:14.550
notifications and by all means join
me at LingQ to learn languages.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:21.240
So we regularly see blog posts,
videos, where people provide

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:25.290
advice on how to learn a language
in a given period of time.

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:26.550
One month...

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:28.440
one month, three months, six months.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:34.680
And typically they have a, you know,
a range of, of, um, tips and tricks

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:38.490
and hacks that will enable you to
learn the language more quickly.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:40.800
I am skeptical.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:47.970
Uh, I'm skeptical because I believe
what Manfred Spitzer said, and

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:49.470
that is that the brain will learn.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:51.600
The brain is a learning machine.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.550
It's a pattern creating machine,
but the brain learns slowly.

00:00:56.610 --> 00:01:00.960
I don't believe that the brain
learns quickly, but you can Google.

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:05.190
And it's interesting to do because
obviously those of us who are

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:08.670
interested in learning languages, we
want to see how other people learn.

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:13.140
We want to, if there are useful tips
and hints, then obviously we want to,

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:15.150
you know, learn from these people.

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:18.690
And there are things that work for some
people that don't work for other people.

00:01:19.815 --> 00:01:23.895
And you'll see that some of the
suggestions are, you know, just

00:01:23.895 --> 00:01:25.005
get out and talk to people.

00:01:25.605 --> 00:01:27.495
Well, it's very difficult to do that.

00:01:27.495 --> 00:01:32.025
If you don't first have the language in
you, you have the more traditionalist

00:01:32.025 --> 00:01:36.795
person who says you must get a good
grammar book, uh, and a good dictionary.

00:01:36.855 --> 00:01:42.285
And I don't find that necessary either
because sure, you'll probably get

00:01:42.285 --> 00:01:45.405
yourself a grammar book or a starter
book, and you'll read through these

00:01:45.405 --> 00:01:47.325
explanations and it won't make much sense.

00:01:47.835 --> 00:01:53.055
Yes, you have to refer back to
some kind of grammar explanation,

00:01:53.055 --> 00:01:54.795
but you can do that at any time.

00:01:54.975 --> 00:01:57.915
It's easily available via Google.

00:01:58.695 --> 00:02:02.265
Uh, and you will become curious
about certain aspects of the

00:02:02.265 --> 00:02:06.375
language after you've had enough
exposure to the language, and

00:02:06.375 --> 00:02:07.795
that's the way we designed LingQ.

00:02:07.815 --> 00:02:10.665
And you can start reading, you
look up words, you start to

00:02:10.665 --> 00:02:14.115
gradually explore and piece together
the, how the language works.

00:02:14.325 --> 00:02:17.115
Then you become curious and
you look up the grammar.

00:02:18.255 --> 00:02:21.405
Some of the other suggestions,
you know, I, I noted some of them.

00:02:21.435 --> 00:02:25.585
Yes, they recommend there are a lot
of apps out there, Duolingo and LingQ,

00:02:25.605 --> 00:02:31.425
of course, and Babel and, and, and,
uh Busuu and however, the fundamental

00:02:31.425 --> 00:02:36.225
process is still one that takes time,
whether you are using these apps or not,

00:02:36.525 --> 00:02:40.875
whether you have a good dictionary or
a good grammar book or not, you still

00:02:40.875 --> 00:02:43.695
have to have the language come in you.

00:02:44.055 --> 00:02:46.395
Some people suggest, you
know, memory techniques.

00:02:47.025 --> 00:02:51.225
Again, some people, if you enjoy,
you know, memory techniques, I must

00:02:51.225 --> 00:02:55.845
confess that I don't I'm I'm not
prepared to invest the time in a

00:02:55.845 --> 00:03:01.114
memory techniques, but some people
are because again, I believe that, you

00:03:01.114 --> 00:03:05.075
know, learning some words in isolation
you've been using memory techniques

00:03:05.644 --> 00:03:08.165
is no substitute for this massive

00:03:08.835 --> 00:03:14.025
input, reading and listening, which is
going to allow my brain to gradually

00:03:14.505 --> 00:03:17.385
get familiar with the language.

00:03:17.925 --> 00:03:21.795
Uh, some people suggest you,
you should have these measurable

00:03:21.795 --> 00:03:24.105
goals and realistic goals.

00:03:24.465 --> 00:03:28.785
And I think that's a good thing,
but I've always found that language

00:03:28.815 --> 00:03:32.035
learning is something that's driven
by your motivation and some days

00:03:32.035 --> 00:03:33.365
you're keener than other days.

00:03:33.915 --> 00:03:38.415
The idea that you should decide that every
day at 10 o'clock in the morning or six

00:03:38.415 --> 00:03:41.775
or whatever, that I'm going to do, this,
that, and the other, it can be part of

00:03:41.775 --> 00:03:43.785
a strategy if you're that way inclined.

00:03:44.145 --> 00:03:45.795
It's not a condition.

00:03:46.185 --> 00:03:49.515
And I don't think it's necessarily
going to move you faster to this

00:03:49.725 --> 00:03:53.655
goal of, of learning the language
in one month or three months.

00:03:54.505 --> 00:04:02.805
Uh, one of the suggestions was that,
you know, You can actually jump from

00:04:03.125 --> 00:04:07.365
B1 on the European framework to C2,
by doing this, that or the other.

00:04:07.665 --> 00:04:09.615
You don't jump in language learning.

00:04:09.615 --> 00:04:15.375
You certainly don't jump from B1 to C2
because actually that's a long, long way.

00:04:15.705 --> 00:04:18.675
And that's like almost, I would say years.

00:04:18.975 --> 00:04:22.915
And to be C2, you have to be living
where the language is spoken.

00:04:22.915 --> 00:04:24.655
You have to be using it a lot.

00:04:24.745 --> 00:04:26.785
You have to have done a lot of reading.

00:04:26.935 --> 00:04:30.415
You have to have put in a lot of
time, over a long period of time.

00:04:30.565 --> 00:04:32.995
You can't just jump
because you start speaking.

00:04:33.085 --> 00:04:36.175
If you don't have the vocabulary and
you don't have the comprehension,

00:04:36.295 --> 00:04:37.495
you aren't going to jump

00:04:37.905 --> 00:04:42.104
to sort of beyond fluency
as it was described.

00:04:42.585 --> 00:04:46.305
Uh, of course we have things
available to us today.

00:04:46.305 --> 00:04:47.925
We can find a tutor online.

00:04:47.925 --> 00:04:54.105
We can have Skype conversations, we can
do all of those things, but we can't

00:04:54.105 --> 00:04:57.735
do those if we don't understand the
language, if we don't have vocabulary.

00:04:58.905 --> 00:05:04.155
And obviously the closer the language
is to one we know the easier it is to

00:05:04.155 --> 00:05:10.035
learn, but to suggest that one shortcut to
learning more quickly is to find cognates,

00:05:10.785 --> 00:05:14.955
cognates are very similar words in
another language, well, that works.

00:05:15.405 --> 00:05:17.985
If you're learning a language,
which has a lot of cognates.

00:05:18.255 --> 00:05:20.685
But if you're learning a
language that has no cognates,

00:05:20.895 --> 00:05:22.724
it doesn't really help very much.

00:05:23.265 --> 00:05:27.555
Generally speaking, the more recognizable
vocabulary there is in the new language,

00:05:27.675 --> 00:05:29.414
the faster you're going to learn.

00:05:30.075 --> 00:05:33.555
Another argument that's put forward
is, you know, learn the most

00:05:33.555 --> 00:05:38.325
important 1000 words, 500 words,
learn the important words upfront.

00:05:38.985 --> 00:05:44.895
In my experience, first of all, having
the most important 500, 1000 words is

00:05:44.895 --> 00:05:49.110
not going to help you if you get into
a genuine, meaningful conversation

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:53.190
with someone because the vocabulary
will go well beyond those words.

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:58.320
And unfortunately, frequency and
vocabulary declines very, very quickly.

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:02.850
So you're still going to have to
chase those less frequent vocabulary

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:07.680
items at some point, if you want to be
genuinely comfortable in the language.

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:11.880
And those very frequent words, they're
going to show up anyway, you don't

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:14.370
really have to focus on learning those.

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:15.990
They all come at you so often.

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:19.140
If you're doing a lot of listening
and reading, those are the ones

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:20.730
that will be the easiest to learn.

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:24.990
You're still gonna have to
pursue the, um, you know, the

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:28.050
less frequently used, uh, words.

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:31.020
And another thing, another thing you
hear is how to sound like a native.

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:36.630
I think if we listen a lot, if we are
attentive to the language, if we are

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:42.940
motivated to, to sort of imitate the
natural native pronunciation, we'll

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:48.300
get better, but we may not ever sound
like a native and it doesn't matter.

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:52.080
In fact, it's interesting, I was
watching a golf tournament and they

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:54.270
interviewed this golfer from Mexico.

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:55.320
I can't remember his name.

00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:58.799
But he had a Spanish accent,
but he spoke excellent English.

00:06:59.039 --> 00:07:04.260
Sometimes if you speak very well and
you have a bit of your native accent

00:07:04.469 --> 00:07:08.940
that almost gives your English or
whatever language, if you're learning

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:11.310
another language, a little bit of charm.

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:16.310
Whereas on the other hand, if you
exaggerate sort of the native twang,

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:22.840
but you don't have the, the, you
know, uh, use of words and the control

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:27.070
of the language that would normally
match that level of pronunciation,

00:07:27.190 --> 00:07:28.719
it may not make a good impression.

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:35.335
So I guess what I wanted to say is,
you know, in, in this idea of studying

00:07:35.354 --> 00:07:40.484
effectively, make sure you expose yourself
to a lot of the language, let the brain

00:07:40.515 --> 00:07:43.065
take its time to learn the language.

00:07:43.094 --> 00:07:46.784
The brain will learn, but it
takes time and don't put pressure

00:07:47.039 --> 00:07:50.849
on yourself to learn it in one
month, three months, six months,

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:54.930
uh, nor is it even certain what
it is you hope to achieve in one

00:07:54.930 --> 00:07:56.340
month, three months, six months.

00:07:56.520 --> 00:08:00.570
Because if it's an a, if it's a new
language, you have no concept of, of

00:08:00.570 --> 00:08:02.430
how much there is in that language.

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:05.219
And typically you'll discover
the deeper you get into it

00:08:05.340 --> 00:08:09.330
that there's just more and more of
it there, which if you're enjoying

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:14.775
the language, it is a delight and
not sort of something you want to

00:08:14.775 --> 00:08:17.085
get past as quickly as possible.

00:08:17.354 --> 00:08:18.525
So there you have it.

00:08:19.484 --> 00:08:21.164
Can you learn language in a month?

00:08:21.674 --> 00:08:22.335
I don't think so.

